  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1 DESCRIPTION...\n\nThe backdoor front has slipped south of most of the forecast \narea this afternoon, but I am not anticipating too much weather \nfrom that feature any longer. It may, very late tonight, provide\na focus for some potential showers as WAA kicks in. While CAMs \ndo show some simulated reflectivity over the area, forecast \nsoundings remain quite dry. So I opted to go with sprinkles \nrather than showers to put some mention of the possibility after\nmidnight. Otherwise it will be cool north of the front, but \nsouth of the front where return flow starts overnight readings \nshould remain in the 50s.\n\nTemps look like a similar split across the forecast area Wed. \nWarm readings south of the boundary and 60s to near 70 north of \nit. Southwest winds may get gusty, especially late in the day, \nup to 25 to 30 mph.\n\nRain showers begin to arrive in the evening and overnight. The \ndiurnal peak of instability is going to line up better for the \nFinger Lakes into the Hudson Valley, with convection dying as it\napproaches the Connecticut River. Much like similar weakening \ncold frontal events this summer, much of the area is looking \nlike a tenth of an inch or less QPF. A few lucky locations may \nsee up to around a half inch. We will take any rainfall, but \nthis does not look like an event that will significant improve \ndrought conditions.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2 DESCRIPTION...\nThe trend is going to be toward drier weather and clearer skies\nThursday as low pressure continues eastward into the Gulf of \nSt. Lawrence sweeping the front through. The front will drop the\ndewpoints back to comfortable levels, but continued \nsouthwesterly flow maintains 850 mb temperatures +12-15 C south \nof the mountains. Mixing profiles from forecast soundings look \ngood, so assuming we do clear out fairly quickly high \ntemperatures in the low to mid-80s are not out of the question. \nNorth of the mountains cooler air is going to be filling in \nbehind the front and capping temperatures in the low to mid-70s.\nA 50kt low level jet also sets up over the region with 850 mb \nwinds more in the 25-30 knot range. As it stands now most \nguidance suggests mixing up to 850 mb looks likely, but \nunsurprisingly only the NAM is convinced of higher potential. \nThat being said I heavily tailored the forecast toward the 90th \npercentile of the NBM to get the 30-35 mph gusts in there that \nwould be expected if we are mixing down 25-30 knot winds. A much\ncooler night is expected as winds go calm and skies trend \nmostly clear. North of the mountains could see temperatures fall\ninto the low to mid-40s, with upper 40s to low 50s in the foot \nhills and the coastal plain and southern New Hampshire ending up\nin the mid to upper 50s.\n\nFriday may also be a fairly breezy day with gradient wind gusts\n20- 25 mph as high pressure begins to build into the area and \nlow pressure continues to slowly move eastward to Atlantic \nCanada. Northwesterly flow will make for a seasonably cool day \nnorth of the mountains as temperatures likely max out in the \n60s. South of the mountains should see a more seasonable day \nwith temperatures in the low to mid 70s. Friday night looks like\na good candidate for radiational cooling as skies remain clear \nand winds calm. I wouldn't be surprised if low temperatures end \nup in the low to mid 40s across most of the area. High pressure \nthan slides overhead Saturday bringing calmer conditions. This \nis likely to be the pick of the week as the upper level ridging \nshould allow for high temperatures in the 70s across the board \nunder clear skies. The high pressure center moves off the coast \nSaturday night and the resulting southwesterly flow keeps low \ntemperatures in the upper 40s and 50s. \n\nSunday into early next week are more unsettled as global models\nwant to bring another front through in the Sunday PM/Monday \nwindow, but global ensembles don't have a very strong signal for\nwidespread precipitation and with how things have gone this \nsummer I don't want to speculate. Therefore, I am fine with the \nNBM advertising slight chance showers south of the mountains \nwith chance showers in the north (aided by the northwesterly \nflow).",
